To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, we can use a variation of the selection sort algorithm called "selection sort with duplicates". This algorithm works by repeatedly finding the maximum (or minimum) element in the unsorted portion of the array and swapping it with the last element of the unsorted portion. We continue this process until all elements have been sorted.
Here's a sample implementation of such a function in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Initialize max1 and max2 variables to store
    // first two largest values encountered so far
    int max1 = INT_MIN, max2 = INT_MIN;

    // Iterate through the array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        // If current element is greater than max1,
        // update max1 and max2
        if (arr[i] > max1 && arr[i] <= max2)
            max2 = arr[i];

        // If current element is greater than max2,
        // but not equal to max1, update max2
        else if (arr[i] > max2 && arr[i] != max1)
            max2 = arr[i];
    }

    return max2;
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` of integers as input and returns the second largest integer value present in the array. It uses two variables `max1` and `max2` to keep track of the first and second largest values encountered so far while iterating through the array.
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), which meets the requirement of having a time complexity of O(n) or less. Additionally, the space complexity of this algorithm is constant, since it only requires a few extra variables to perform the necessary operations.
